    "reading": "Sleep supports several processes associated with learning and memory. During the day, people encounter information that may later become easier to retrieve after a period of rest. Researchers have proposed that some stages of sleep contribute to organising or strengthening newly formed memories. Yet the relationship is complex. The amount and quality of sleep vary among individuals, and stress, health and prior knowledge can influence test performance. In a laboratory study, participants learned a list of unfamiliar words. One group rested overnight before a follow-up test, while another was tested after a long period awake. The rested group recalled more words on average. The investigators noted, however, that the controlled laboratory setting did not capture the full range of real-world learning conditions. The results suggested a useful association, not a universal guarantee.",
